@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "1. Put a player on screen") {
        Start with a square you can see and change. Along the way, you will learn what an entity is, what a component adds to it, and how SwiftUI and SpriteUI work together.

        Before you begin, add SpriteUI to a SwiftUI app as shown in <doc:getting-started>. Each code stage is a complete `ContentView.swift`. Use one stage at a time and keep your app's existing entry point.

        @Image(source: "spriteui-first-scene-03-preview.svg", alt: "A preview of the scene you will build in this lesson.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Give an entity something to draw") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Think of an entity as an object in your game. On its own it has a name and a position, but no appearance. Adding a ``SpriteComponent`` makes our player a cyan square.

            ``GameView`` provides the game world. The setup closure adds the player to that world so the renderer can draw it. The `FirstSceneModel` holds on to the player so later buttons can change the same object.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Run the first stage. Find the `Entity` declaration in `FirstSceneModel`, then the call to `content.add(player)` in `install`. Together, these create the player and put it on screen.

                `@State` keeps the scene model while SwiftUI redraws the view. `@MainActor` keeps its entity work on the same actor as the game view.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-first-scene-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-first-scene-01-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Change `.cyan` to `.pink` and run again. Then try changing the size from `[36, 36]` to `[60, 24]`. You are describing the player's appearance through component data; you do not need to create or configure an `SKSpriteNode` yourself.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Arrange the scene") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A second entity can mark a destination. Giving each entity its own position arranges the scene without changing how either sprite is drawn.

            The center of this game area is `[0, 0]`. Positive x goes right; positive y goes up. These entities have no custom parent, so their positions are measured from the scene origin.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use stage two. The player starts at `[-80, 0]` and the orange marker at `[80, 0]`. Run the app and check that the square is left of the marker.

                The scene model now retains both entities, and setup adds both to the world.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-first-scene-02.swift", previousFile: "spriteui-first-scene-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-first-scene-02-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Move the marker to `[80, 60]`. It should appear above and to the right of the center. This is the coordinate system you will use for movement, walls, and cameras throughout the course.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Change the existing player") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Once the scene exists, you usually want to change its entities rather than build them again. A SwiftUI button changes the model's `usesWarmColor` property. The `GameView` update closure reads that property and changes the player's sprite component.

            Setup creates the scene. The update closure applies app state to it. In the next lesson, a system will handle changes that depend on time.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use the final stage and press Change player color. The player changes color while the marker stays orange. Find the typed component access, `scene.player.components[SpriteComponent.self]`, which lets the closure edit the player's appearance.

                @Code(name: "ContentView.swift", file: "spriteui-first-scene-03.swift", previousFile: "spriteui-first-scene-02.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "spriteui-first-scene-03-preview.svg", alt: "Expected appearance of the running example.")
                }
            }
            @Step {
                Toggle the button several times. The player stays in the same place because you keep changing one entity instead of adding a new one.

                You can now create an entity, give it an appearance, and change its component data. Continue with <doc:moving-with-systems-and-input> to make it move.
            }
        }
    }
}
